                <pb facs="0396" n="284" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg284"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7132: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-29T12:00:00+08:00">June 29,
                        1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 5:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>324. The following two telegrams have been received from Harbin: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 28, 10 a.m. Unconfirmed reports believed to be true state that a
                            surveying party of the Kwantung Army was on June 24 attacked and two
                            members kidnapped by Outer Mongolian troops who allegedly crossed the
                            boundary 80 kilometers southeast of Gandjur. The Army regards it
                            seriously and has proposed to settle the matter at the present Manchuli
                            conference if the Mongolian delegates can receive appropriate
                            instructions from their Government.”</p>
                        <p>“June 29, 2 p.m. Referring to my despatch No. 86, of June 14, a Japanese
                            official has confirmed a report stating that the ‘Manchukuo’ delegates
                            have received instructions to accept the limited agenda desired by the
                            Mongols and that consequently general questions will not be taken up at
                            least for the present. A possible break-up of the conference has thus
                            apparently been averted.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>The local Japanese Embassy states that it has no new information with regard to
                    these matters.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
